# Expose RAPL package energy counters to unprivileged readers.
#
# The kernel ships /sys/class/powercap/intel-rapl:*/energy_uj as 0400 root-only.
# sysmon-agent's systemd unit runs the agent under an unprivileged User= with
# NoNewPrivileges=true, so without this rule CPU package power degrades with a
# "not readable" error on an otherwise healthy bare-metal host. Power readings
# are low-sensitivity, so world-readable (0444) is the intended relaxation.
#
# Install with:
#   sudo install -m 0644 scripts/udev/99-powercap-rapl.rules /etc/udev/rules.d/
#   sudo udevadm trigger --subsystem-match=powercap
#
# The rule matches ACTION=="add|change" on purpose. Boot fires "add", but
# `udevadm trigger` defaults to "change" -- an add-only rule installs cleanly,
# reports nothing, and silently leaves energy_uj at 0400 until the next reboot.
#
# Hosts that expose no RAPL interface at all (some VMs, locked-down BIOS) are
# unaffected: the glob is simply a no-op there.
SUBSYSTEM=="powercap", ACTION=="add|change", \
  RUN+="/bin/sh -c 'chmod 0444 /sys/class/powercap/intel-rapl:*/energy_uj 2>/dev/null; true'"
